# L5R 5E - FIELDS OF VICTORY: THE BADGER CLAN (LORE)

**Version:** 0.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Fields of Victory (Chapter 2)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## THE BADGER CLAN

Every samurai bears a duty to protect the Empire from threats both internal and external. For the Badger Clan, a Minor Clan dwelling within the Great Wall of the North mountains along Rokugan's northwestern frontier, that duty defines every facet of their existence. They guard the few passable mountain trails -- most critically the Path of Woe -- serving as sentries and lookouts against gaijin horseback raiders from beyond the border. From childhood, every Badger is trained to be watchful and ready to raise the alarm at the first sign of outsiders in the mountains. This perpetual wariness, combined with the harsh and inhospitable terrain of their homeland, has forged the legendary toughness for which the clan is known.

Visitors who expect to find only grim, hardworking bushi offering little hospitality are in for a surprise. The Badger are an isolated people, always eager for news from the rest of Rokugan. Once a visitor is judged friend rather than foe, the hosting castle erupts into celebration -- feasting, drinking, music, dancing, and competitive games, especially those involving wrestling and feats of strength. Travelers to Badger lands are rare, and the clan makes the most of each one. Most return home with tales of samurai twice the size of ordinary bushi, children who scramble up and down mountains with the agility of goats, and appalling table manners.

The Badger have never forgotten their Crab heritage. They consider the Crab distant kin who share a common goal, though Crab warriors are quick to note that while they fight the Shadowlands daily, the Badger face their enemies far less frequently. And while Badger territory is cold and unforgiving, it is hardly as perilous as the border of the Shadowlands. This difference may explain why, despite their many shared qualities, the Badger maintain a more jovial temperament than their Crab cousins.

---

## THE FOUNDING

In the second century, a young Crab warrior named Hida Domogu loved sumai wrestling above all other pursuits. He excelled at the art and won great glory within his clan, but his victories bred pride. Seeking to temper that pride, his family sent him on his musha shugyo -- a warrior's pilgrimage -- with instructions to travel as widely through Rokugan as he could during his year away. He took the name Ichiro for his journey, deliberately choosing such a common name as an early act of humility.

Outside Crab lands, other samurai saw only a ronin -- someone presumed crude and uncultured, whose reputation as a wrestler was unknown. Ichiro sought competitions to prove himself, but discovered that in the rest of Rokugan, sumai was not as celebrated as arts like iaijutsu and ikebana, in which he had far less skill. Humbled in earnest, he focused on his travels.

Eventually Ichiro reached what he believed to be the northwestern edge of Rokugan, in Lion territory, only to learn of a village deeper within the rugged border mountains. Intrigued by people who chose to live at the very edge of civilization, he made his way into the peaks -- and promptly fell into a cunningly concealed pit trap in one of the passes. Villagers armed with nothing more than farming tools captured him.

The villagers were not all peasants. Among them were ronin, bandits, and a large-statured woman whose features were gaijin though she spoke Rokugani. Viewing Ichiro as a common outcast samurai, they took him in and allowed him to learn their methods of defending their lands from outsiders. In turn, he taught them sumai, and before long a thriving wrestling tradition took root in the mountains.

Ichiro soon became a valued member of the village, which proved fortunate when the sounds of horns and hoofbeats echoed through the passes. Scouts reported a massive force of mounted gaijin descending from a distant mountain pass -- a force so large it might have been several tribes working in concert. If the horde was not stopped, the unprepared Rokugani defenders to the south would suffer grievous losses and leave the Empire vulnerable to further incursions. The villagers, even with their traps and defenses, would be quickly overrun, and they prepared to abandon their homes.

But Ichiro had other plans. He was Hida, and the Hida are always ready to defend. Armed now with the skills the villagers had taught him, he organized multiple squads to slow the invaders and funnel them into narrow pathways where a handful of defenders could hold off many. The squads employed drums to communicate, using rhythms so natural-sounding that the invaders could not discern their origin or meaning. The gaijin riders found their pre-scouted passages newly filled with iron spikes, while rocks rained down on them from above. Routes grew progressively more treacherous and unstable, crowding the horde into a single killing ground.

As the horses slowed and the drumbeats swelled, panic spread through the gaijin ranks. Those who desperately tried to climb free reached the clifftops only to be hurled back down to their deaths. The mountains themselves seemed to shake as the drumbeats echoed and boulders rolled down the slopes into the trapped horde. Ichiro then led his sumai warriors into a final crushing assault, pulling riders from their horses. The surviving gaijin who escaped would later tell of how the mountains themselves, awakened and angered by the pounding drums, rose up and destroyed their army.

Ichiro was hailed for his efforts and named the new village leader. He declared that the final pass where they had routed the gaijin would be known as the Path of Woe, for it had proved -- and would continue to prove -- woe to any who thought to invade Rokugan. By the time Akodo scouts arrived with a Lion army, the villagers had already improved their defenses in anticipation of a second wave. When the Emperor heard the tale, he elevated the villagers to Minor Clan status. Ichiro was made their daimyo, and the new Badger Clan was entrusted with defending Rokugan's border in those mountains. The Lion considered this a slight but could not openly disagree with the Emperor's decree. In the years that followed, the Badger built fortresses and set traps, and the Emperor was pleased with their progress. So began the vigil of the Badger.

---

## CHARACTER AND CULTURE

When left to their own devices -- which is most of the time, given their isolation -- the Badger are industrious and focused. Their bushi train hard and work alongside peasants at the grueling task of mining, providing ore for the clan's engineers and smiths. Artisans produce weapons, armor, tools, traps, and innovative contraptions unknown to other clans. Children wrestle each other when not occupied with chores, while older Badger tend Mulberry Valley and its silkworms, creating delicate silken goods for trade.

The ratio of samurai to peasants among the Badger is unusually balanced. Badger samurai do not shy away from hard labor that other clans would leave entirely to the lower classes. Peasants who are not mining typically work in agriculture, specializing in intensive farming of the clan's rich volcanic soil to feed a perpetually hungry populace.

The entire clan is organized with defense in mind. Most bushi serve regular patrol duty throughout their lives, and the Badger have built their fortresses at the most vulnerable points along the passes. Each clan member is assigned a role suited to their individual strengths. Many Badger are naturally large and physically powerful, especially the warriors and guards. The fastest samurai train as scouts, who travel the mountains silently in small groups and periodically venture into gaijin lands to watch for growing threats such as a massing coalition of Ujik or other would-be invaders. Those with inquisitive and creative minds become inventors and smiths. All Badger samurai have important roles to play in the defense of Rokugan.

### Sumai Wrestling

Sumai is more than a sport among the Badger -- it holds deep spiritual and competitive significance. Badger samurai enjoy many competitions of strength and martial prowess, a necessity since they are rarely able to prove themselves on an actual battlefield. Bushi compete in sumai matches as part of their training, but the Badger also hold sumai bouts before important ceremonies to impress local kami and ward off malevolent spirits. The most successful wrestlers are considered favored by the kami and treated with great respect. The reigning Badger sumai champion is regarded as the spiritual equal of the Badger Clan Champion, though the sumai champion's authority is limited to the spiritual sphere.

Each year the Badger hold the Great Games at the Castle of the Badger, inviting representatives of every clan in the Empire. Sumai is the main event, followed by days of revelry. The tournament is usually poorly attended, perhaps because the Badger are so remote, or perhaps because no Badger sumai champion has yet been defeated by a contender from outside the clan. The Badger follow the same sumai rules as the Crab.

### Silk Production

The Badger cultivate a rare variety of mulberry tree in a sun-warmed volcanic valley, supporting a thriving silkworm farm that has grown into an important source of clan wealth. Badger silk is particularly fine and delicate -- even the Crane are rumored to purchase it raw, though no Crane would admit to using such an uncultured source. Painting the silk is considered an appropriate pursuit for elder Badger samurai, whose mountain-landscape designs adorn the halls of the Castle of the Badger and other homes, enrobe their shugenja, or are sent to the Emperor as part of the rice tax. These designs, inspired by the jagged beauty of the volcanoes and peaks the Badger call home, are regarded as splendid curiosities at the Imperial Court.

The Badger guard their silk farm jealously, only permitting accompanied visitors to enter the valley or caves involved in production. The gaijin beyond the mountains are known to place great value on silk, and after an incident in which a visiting impostor posing as a monk attempted to smuggle silkworms north, the clan has been especially wary of plots from both gaijin and other Rokugani.

---

## MILITARY AND TRADE

Well-placed traps, checked regularly by sappers, are only one element of the Badger's defensive strategy. The clan maintains an uneasy alliance with the Lion, whose scorn of Badger manners and customs is thinly veiled by the courtesy necessary for trade. Badger weapons may lack the reputation or beauty of more renowned blades, but they are reliable in battle and inexpensive. Badger armor is likewise plain but practical. Crucially, Badger forges can produce both in large numbers, making them appealing to the Lion for equipping their vast armies of ashigaru. It is the clan's more complex creations that the Lion truly prize, however -- items such as mechanical limbs with fully articulated digits, designed to replace those lost in battle. Only the design of their traps do the Badger refuse to share, as these devices provide crucial security in their mountain home and could be devastating if turned against a castle or camp within the Empire.

Badger weapons often serve dual purposes. Their distinctive sapper axe, for example, is half hand axe and half hammer, with a smooth wooden handle that doubles as a drumstick. Ichiro-made weapons typically bear the Ichiro pattern as their only decoration and tend to be stronger and more durable than comparable arms forged elsewhere in the Empire.

### Drum Communication

The use of drums pervades Badger life. Beyond their role in rituals and ceremonies, drums form the backbone of the clan's communication network. Messages can be sent from one Badger fortress to another using drum signals, and each patrol includes a drummer who can sound a warning that echoes through the mountains. These rhythmic messages can convey a great deal of information, and most Badger samurai can interpret the signals hidden within them. Within the Castle of the Badger and some of the larger homes, drums accompany dances and other entertainments. All Badger know there are certain places where it is unsafe to use a drum, where loud sounds might trigger landslides. Should the clan ever be overrun, drummers know how to create deliberate rockfalls to destroy passes and trap enemies within the mountains as a final, desperate measure.

### Trade with Outsiders

The Badger consider trade important, not least for the information traders bring with them. Given that some of the original villagers had gaijin blood, the clan has maintained discreet trade relationships with certain gaijin who have demonstrated peaceful intentions. This is kept secret from the rest of Rokugan, or at the very least handled with great circumspection.

In addition to rough grains such as millet and buckwheat, the Badger cultivate native fungi and catch fish in mountain rivers. They grow plum and mulberry trees in areas warmed by volcanic activity, producing plum wine and silk for both personal use and trade.

---

## SHUGENJA

Badger shugenja are rare, so any clan member who shows sensitivity to the kami is encouraged to apprentice with an elder shugenja and share their duties as quickly as possible. These shugenja often possess an affinity for Earth or Fire, surrounded as they are by mountains and dormant volcanoes. Their duties extend to warning the clan of approaching earthquakes, landslides, and the very infrequent volcanic eruptions. They can influence these natural events, and tales are told of entire armies defeated by tremors encouraged by Badger shugenja.

Shugenja also guide mining efforts, using their connection to the kami of metal and stone to direct miners toward the richest ore veins and away from areas where excavation might weaken the mountainside -- or toward areas where such weakening could be turned into defensive traps against approaching enemies.

Badger shugenja share their spiritual duties with the highest-ranking sumai wrestlers. These wrestlers learn how to conduct ceremonies and rituals, though they lack a shugenja's ability to communicate directly with the kami. Such sumai masters often serve as priests when no shugenja, priest, or monk is available.

---

## LANDS OF THE BADGER

The entirety of Badger territory lies within the Great Wall of the North mountains, to the west of Dragon lands. The only known access to Badger lands from within Rokugan is through the Path of Woe, which passes all the way through the mountains and out of the Empire. Since defending this pass is the clan's fundamental purpose, nearly every Badger holding sits in a strategic position along it, its people ready to repel invaders at a moment's notice.

The terrain within the mountains is largely cold and unforgiving, but the Badger apply their ingenuity to make the most of every resource -- each patch of volcanic soil, each ore-rich vein in the rock, each hot spring and sunny valley. Every building and mine is carefully planned to make defending the surrounding area easier or to make the pass more treacherous for enemies to navigate.

### Castle of the Badger (Shiro Ichiro)

The home of the Ichiro daimyo, the Castle of the Badger is an enormous fortress built high on the mountainside, overlooking the widest and most open section of the Path of Woe. Situated roughly at the center of Badger territory, it is the only true castle the clan possesses. The Great Games are held annually within its vast courtyard and on the nearby mountainside. It has seldom-used guest quarters for competitors, all equipped with heated baths, and a grand dining hall where silk hangings adorn the walls. Mountain delicacies grace the Badger table during celebrations.

The castle was built near fertile ground suitable for farming, so that during an invasion the peasant farmers can retreat to safety within its walls. The Badger have laid out a number of narrow paths and constructed several underground passages leading to the castle, all of which can be collapsed or blocked if necessary. In the lowest levels of the castle are several forges and workshops where Badger engineers, inventors, weaponsmiths, and blacksmiths design, produce, and test all manner of items. More dangerous experiments are usually conducted outside the castle, where strange black marks across the nearby rock face bear evidence of their work. Occasionally a new trap or experimental device detonates within the castle walls, but Badger architecture is sturdy enough that the damage is minimal.

### Badger's Scowl

This fierce-looking craggy volcano has long been dormant but remains a source of great benefit to the Badger. It supplies hot springs and steam vents. The hot springs provide the Badger with therapeutic baths, while the steam vents serve as a heat source for Fureheshu Mine.

### Fureheshu Mine

The reinforced edifice of Fureheshu Mine is irregular and jagged in its construction, designed so that the building appears to be part of the surrounding mountainside. It is the second-largest Badger holding, serving as the home of the Fureheshu daimyo and doubling as a fortress in times of need. Chunks of rock beneath its protruding towers have been half-broken so they can be easily dislodged and dropped onto any attackers who struggle up the narrow paths toward the mine. The area also contains the Badger sumai training ring and a brewery that produces a famously potent plum wine. The mine itself holds veins rich with iron ore, and the demanding labor required to extract it from the earth is often incorporated into both sumai and samurai training.

### Mulberry Valley

When a silk merchant chose to stay with the Badger rather than face his debts at home, nobody expected him to establish a successful silkworm farm in the cold mountains. However, one sun-warmed volcanic valley turned out to be ideal for growing a rare variety of mulberry tree. Generations after the merchant's death, silk production has become an important pillar of Badger wealth. Children help feed the silkworms in their caves, bringing in baskets of mulberry leaves from the valley, while talented Badger samurai paint striking mountain landscapes on the finished silk before it is traded to other clans.

### Mysterious Shrine

A small shrine nestled in the mountains near Badger's Scowl offers no indication as to which kami might inhabit it. Badger patrols sometimes come across it seemingly by accident and silently pay their respects before moving on. Only the Sumai Champion visits with deliberate purpose, to sit in contemplation. Visits from outsiders are exceedingly rare, though occasionally one appears under escort to offer respectful prayers. Rumors abound as to the shrine's true nature -- some say a lost Kami's grave lies here, others that it is the site where the first silk-producing butterflies were discovered, and still others whisper that an oni is buried underneath, one that only a Sumai Champion could defeat should it ever emerge.
